Yes. The inverse of wanting is often wanting to be wanted.

What you are describing is not just attraction. It is the felt evidence of reciprocity:

I choose you
↔
you choose me

That can show up as enthusiasm, initiative, attention, warmth, anticipation, effort, curiosity, touch, or simply the sense that the other person is moving toward you rather than merely allowing you to move toward them.

The important distinction is that those signals are evidence, not mind-reading. A person can be reserved and still care deeply; someone can be expressive without deeper commitment. So the strongest signal is usually a pattern over time:

words + initiative + consistency + voluntary effort + responsiveness

When those converge, the experience becomes much more than desire. It becomes mutual selection.

That may be the cleaner term for what you are reaching for: not merely passion, but being actively chosen in return.